Dear Ista,
Thanks for your answer! I find that the
solution you give seem to apply the same vector of weights (corresponding to
lalonde1$w) to both data sets. What I need however is a little trickier: as I
am doing matching on several imputed data sets, I obtain a different vector of
weights for each data set. I thus need to apply to each data set its own vector
of weights (here lalonde$w and lalonde1$w). Sorry if that was not clear in the
previous post. So I tried with different forms of weights combining the two
vectors: a long vector in the environment, list or data frame, but none
works. The only solution that partly works is weights=”w”, wich applies the
proper weights to each data set. It works perfectly without robust = T. With
robust = T it fits the model properly to each data sets. So the only problem is
with summary() which I think is when std errors are calculated (I guess it may be because the robust = T also use weights?). The solution I
see (maybe there are others) is that the summary() would have to be slightly
modified to accommodate robust=T. Unfortunately that is above my
competences…  Below is the syntax to
illustrate the different points in the present post.
